    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d39-a1">

                <pb facs="0146" n="115" xml:id="pg115"/>


                <head>Table Prepared in the Department of State<anchor n="3" xml:id="d39-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian">undated</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <table xml:id="table006">
                    <head>SALT Dismantlements Since 1972</head>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>U.S. Launchers</cell>
                        <cell>USSR Launchers</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>ICBM</cell>
                        <cell>18</cell>
                        <cell>229</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>SLBM</cell>
                        <cell>128</cell>
                        <cell>221</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Total</cell>
                        <cell>146</cell>
                        <cell>450</cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <list>
                    <label>○</label>
                    <item>The USSR also has converted 800
                        launchers of SS–9s and SS–11s into launchers for SS–17s, SS–18s, and SS–19s.
                        Without SALT I the Soviets could have
                        retained the old ICBMs as new ones entered the force.</item>
                    <label>○</label>
                    <item>SALT I does not limit heavy bombers. No
                        formal procedures exist for the dismantlement, destruction, or conversion of
                        heavy bombers accountable under SALT II.
                        The U.S. has dismantled 42 B–52s which
                        remain accountable under SALT II. The
                        Soviets have converted 2 Bison bombers to tankers, and deactivated 35
                        Bisons, which also remain accountable under SALT II.</item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
